            <section class="mt-content-container">
                <p>Drawing Lewis structures, or line-bond structures, and describing delocalized electrons by resonance and resonance hybrid is a graphical way of representing organic molecules. Names are given to things to represent them in words. The same applies to organic molecules; they are assigned names to represent them in words. For example, ethanol is a molecule that has the following Lewis structure: \(\ce{H-\overset{\overset{\huge{H}}|}{\underset{\underset{\huge{H}} |}{C}}-\overset{\!\large\:\cdot\:\!\cdot}{\underset{\!\large\:\cdot\:\!\cdot}{O}}-H}\), or the following line bond structure: <img alt="clipboard_e09f347238514d856db92a6a48580b093.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488372/clipboard_e09f347238514d856db92a6a48580b093.png?revision=1" />. Before systematic nomenclature was developed, organic compounds were known by common names. The International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC) has developed a systematic nomenclature, called <span style="box-sizing:border-box;margin:0px;padding:0px;">the<strong> IUPAC</strong></span><strong> nomenclature</strong> or <strong>systematic nomenclature</strong> of organic compounds. Systematic names are assigned so that each organic molecule has a unique name, such that one may be able to draw the structure from the name and deduce the name from the structure. </p>

<p>Systematic nomenclature is based on the name of the \(\ce{C}\) chain and the name of the reactive part of the molecule, called a functional group. Chemical reactions involve the making and breaking of chemical bonds that usually take place in the functional groups. Therefore, the making and breaking of chemical bonds, followed by functional groups, are described next before describing IUPAC nomenclature.</p>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/02:_Representing_organic_structures/2.03:_Organic_nomenclature__-alkanes" class="mt-section" id="section_1"><span id="Basic_chemical_reaction_types"></span><h2 class="editable">Basic chemical reaction types</h2>

<p>Chemical reactions, i.e., making and breaking of chemical bonds, are divided into two major classes, homolytic and heterolytic bond making and breaking, as described next.</p>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/02:_Representing_organic_structures/2.03:_Organic_nomenclature__-alkanes" class="mt-section" id="section_2"><span id="Homolytic_bond_making_and_breaking"></span><h3 class="editable">Homolytic bond making and breaking</h3>

<p>A covalent bond is formed by sharing valence electrons, one from each atom, e.g., as in the formation of \(\ce{Cl2}\) molecule by the following reaction:   \[\ce{{\LARGE :}\overset{\Huge{\cdot\cdot}}{\underset{\Huge{\cdot\cdot}}{Cl}}{\LARGE\cdot} + {\LARGE \cdot}\overset{\Huge{\cdot\cdot}}{\underset{\Huge{\cdot\cdot}}{Cl}}{\LARGE :} -&gt; {\LARGE :}\overset{\Huge{\cdot\cdot}}{\underset{\Huge{\cdot\cdot}}{Cl}}-\overset{\Huge{\cdot\cdot}}{\underset{\Huge{\cdot\cdot}}{Cl}}{\LARGE :}  }\nonumber\] Movement of valence electrons in chemical reactions is shown by curly arrows \(\huge\curvearrowright\). The tail of the arrow is placed where the electrons to be moved are located, and the head points where the electrons move to (\(\text {tail} {\huge\curvearrowright}\text head\)). Regular curly arrows \(\huge\curvearrowright\) show movement of two electrons, a lone pair or a \(\pi\)-bonding pair, and curly half-headed arrows <img alt="clipboard_ed3ec0933c4f5b873347b61ccb3545503.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488374/clipboard_ed3ec0933c4f5b873347b61ccb3545503.png?revision=1" /> show movement of a single electron. </p>

<section class="box-emphasis">
<p>Bond formation, in which both atoms contribute one electron to form a covalent bond, is known <span style="box-sizing:border-box;margin:0px;padding:0px;">as<strong> homolytic</strong></span><strong> bond formation</strong>. e.g.,</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="clipboard_e91ec151d1696a97a198307a409da3c81.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488393/clipboard_e91ec151d1696a97a198307a409da3c81.png?revision=1" />.</p>

<p>The reverse of it, i.e., bond breaking, in which both fragments receive one electron, is called <strong>homolytic bond breaking</strong>, e.g.,</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="clipboard_eb08690192f63d54fd1d41be43bfca008.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488394/clipboard_eb08690192f63d54fd1d41be43bfca008.png?revision=1" />.</p>
</section>

<p>Energy released during homolytic bond making is the same amount of energy needed for homolytic bond breaking, which is called the bond dissociation energy (BDE). </p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/02:_Representing_organic_structures/2.03:_Organic_nomenclature__-alkanes" class="mt-section" id="section_3"><span id="Heterolytic_bond_making_and_breaking"></span><h3 class="editable">Heterolytic bond making and breaking</h3>

<section class="box-emphasis">
<p>Bond formation in which both the bonding electrons are donated by one atom is known as <strong>heterolytic bond formation</strong>, e.g., \[\ce{(CH3)3\overset{+}{C}\! \!^\overset{\Huge\curvearrowleft\;\;\;\;\;\;}{ {\;\;\;+\LARGE :}\overset{\Huge{\cdot\cdot}}{\underset{\Huge{\cdot\cdot}}{Cl}}{\LARGE :}^{-} }  -&gt; (CH3)3C-\overset{\Huge{\cdot\cdot}}{\underset{\Huge{\cdot\cdot}}{Cl}}{\LARGE :}} \nonumber \] Reverse of it, i.e., bond breaking, in which both the bonding electrons are received by one of the fragments, is called <strong>heterolytic bond breaking</strong>, e.g., \[\ce{(CH3)3C\;\; ^\overset{\Huge\curvearrowright}{\!\!\!\!\!\!\!{\Large -}\;\;\;\;}\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!{\LARGE :}{\underset{\Huge{\cdot\cdot}}{Cl}{\LARGE :}} -&gt; (CH3)3\overset{+}{C} + {{\LARGE :}\overset{\Huge{\cdot\cdot}}{\underset{\Huge{\cdot\cdot}}{Cl}}{\LARGE :}^{-} }   }\nonumber\]</p>
</section>

<p>Energy needed for heterolytic bond breaking is not the same as in homolytic bond breaking, because the products of the two are different. Heterolytic bond breaking typically occurs in polar bonds, where the bonding electrons are transferred to the more electronegative atom, whereas homolytic bond breaking usually takes place in nonpolar bonds. This is because the atomic orbital of the more electronegative atom is closer in energy to the molecular orbital. In contrast, in a nonpolar bond, the atomic orbitals of both atoms are about the same distance away from the molecular orbital in terms of energy, as shown in Figure \(\PageIndex{1}\). Furthermore, most chemical reactions in biological systems, as well as in the laboratory, occur in solution, where polar solvents stabilize charges, making heterolytic processes more favorable. Therefore, the reactive sites in organic molecules, called functional groups, are usually associated with polar bonds between \(\ce{C}\) and heteroatoms like \(\ce{O}\), \(\ce{N}\), halogens, etc. </p>

<figure><img alt="clipboard_e80ea156bd15972d21a0c5b9b3721f153.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487124/clipboard_e80ea156bd15972d21a0c5b9b3721f153.png?revision=1" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{1}\): Depiction of a MO diagram of a nonpolar covalent bond (left) and a polar covalent bond (right). In both cases, a bond is a shared pair of electrons. (Copyright; Krishnavedala, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons)</figcaption>
</figure>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/02:_Representing_organic_structures/2.03:_Organic_nomenclature__-alkanes" class="mt-section" id="section_4"><span id="Functional_groups"></span><h3 class="editable">Functional groups </h3>

<section class="box-definition">
<p>A <strong>functional group</strong> is a group of atoms in an organic molecule that is associated with a characteristic set of chemical properties.</p>
</section>

<p> A functional group usually contains polar bonds, e.g., alkyl halide \(\ce{(R)3\overset{\delta{+}}{C}{-}\overset{\delta{-}}{Cl}}\), alcohol  \(\ce{(R)3\overset{\delta{+}}{C}{-}\overset{\delta{-}}{O}{-}\overset{\delta{+}}{H}}\), carbonyl  \(\ce{(R)2\overset{\delta{+}}{C}{=}\overset{\delta{-}}{O}}\), etc., or a combination of these, e.g., carboxylic acid  \(\ce{R-\!\!{\overset{\overset{\huge\enspace\!{\overset{\Large{\delta{-}}}{O}}}|\!\!|\enspace}{\underset{\delta{+}}{C}}}\!\!-\overset{\delta{-}}{O}-\overset{\delta{+}}{H}}\), which is a combination of a carbonyl and an alcohol groups, and acid halid \(\ce{-\!\!{\overset{\overset{\huge\enspace\!{\overset{\Large{\delta{-}}}{O}}}|\!\!|\enspace}{\underset{\delta{+}}{C}}}\!\!-\overset{\delta{-}}{Cl}}\), which is a combination of a carbonyl and an alkyl halide groups, etc., where \(\ce{R}\) represents the rest of the organic molecule. Functional groups usually react through heterolytic chemical processes, known as the polar reaction mechanism.</p>

<p>Organic compounds containing only \(\ce{C}\) and \(\ce{H}\) atoms are called hydrocarbons. Hydrocarbons that have only single bonds are called alkanes, those having at least one double \(\ce{C=C}\) bond are called alkenes, and those having at least one triple \(\ce{C\equiv C}\) bond are called alkynes. The \(\pi\)-bonds in \(\ce{C=C}\) and \(\ce{C\equiv C}\) are also called unsaturation. Since \(\pi\)-bond is weaker than a \(\sigma\)-bond and its electrons are relatively less tightly held by the nuclei, they are easily donated in chemical reactions. Therefore,  \(\ce{C=C}\) and \(\ce{C\equiv C}\) are functional groups. Alkanes are the least reactive class of organic compounds as they do not have functional groups. Alkanes typically react through a homolytic chemical process, known as the free radical mechanism.</p>

<p>The structure and names of the most common functional groups in organic compounds are described in the next section about organic nomenclature. Further details are provided in the next chapters.</p>

</div></div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/02:_Representing_organic_structures/2.03:_Organic_nomenclature__-alkanes" class="mt-section" id="section_5"><span id="Introduction_to_IUPAC_nomenclature"></span><h2 class="editable">Introduction to IUPAC nomenclature</h2>

<p>This introduction to the IUPAC nomenclature is extracted from: IUPAC, Pure Appl. Chem. 2020, <a title="https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0104" href="https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0104" target="_blank" rel="external noopener nofollow" class="link-https">https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-<br />
0104</a>. The IUPAC names are generated using <a title="https://www.acdlabs.com/resources/free-chemistry-software-apps/chemsketch-freeware/" href="https://www.acdlabs.com/resources/free-chemistry-software-apps/chemsketch-freeware/" target="_blank" rel="external noopener nofollow" class="link-https">ACD/ChemSketch (freeware)</a>. The structures are drawn using  <a title="https://www.kingdraw.com/indexen?name=download" href="https://www.kingdraw.com/indexen?name=download" target="_blank" rel="external noopener nofollow" class="link-https">KingDraw for PC</a>.</p>

<p>The structure of the IUPAC nomenclature of an organic compound, with the parts labeled, is shown in Figure \(\PageIndex{2}\) and described next.</p>

<ol start="1" style="list-style-type: lower-alpha;">
    <li>It is the parent chain, which is usually the longest carbon chain on which a characteristic functional group is present.</li>
    <li>It is a suffix representing unsaturation, i.e., it tells whether \(\ce{C}\) atoms have only single bonds or also have double or triple bonds.</li>
    <li>Represents the suffix of the senior functional group.</li>
    <li>It is an alphabetical list of prefixes representing branches on the parent chain or non-senior functional groups, collectively called substituents.</li>
    <li>The locant that tells the position on the parent chain of a substituent is listed next to it; the numbers are listed in increasing order, separated by commas, and are distinguished from alphabets by hyphens.</li>
    <li>It is a multiplier telling how many times the substituted attached to it is repeated; the multiplier is not counted in alphabetization of the substituents, and </li>
    <li>is a stereodescriptor preceded by the locant number of the stereogenic center if present in a molecule and enclosed in brackets. When parts of a name belong together, they are enclosed in brackets in the nesting order {[( )]}. </li>
</ol>

<figure><img alt="clipboard_e3d6e90decce88708d868905f6fbaceeb.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488413/clipboard_e3d6e90decce88708d868905f6fbaceeb.png?revision=1" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{2}\): Structure of the IUPAC nomenclature is illustrated with components labeled: a) is the parent chain, b) is suffix indicating unsaturation, c) is the suffix representing senior group, d) are prefixes representing substituents listed in alphabetic order, e) are locant numbers separated from each other by comma and from the alphabets by hyphens, f) is the multiplier, g) is is the stereodescriptor enclosed in brackets. (Copyright; Public domain).</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>Naming simple organic compounds needed in this course is described in the next section, starting with hydrocarbons, followed by compounds containing non-characteristic functional groups, and finally, compounds containing characteristic functional groups. Note: stereodescripters are ignored in this chapter and will be described and added to the nomenclature in the next chapter.</p>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/02:_Representing_organic_structures/2.03:_Organic_nomenclature__-alkanes" class="mt-section" id="section_6"><span id="Nomenclature_of_hydrocarbons"></span><h3 class="editable">Nomenclature of hydrocarbons</h3>

<p><img alt="Classification of hydrocations illustrated" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488417/clipboard_e278f43b38d52e59943697a767f7372dc.png?revision=1" />Hydrocarbons are organic compounds containing only \(\ce{C}\) and \(\ce{H}\) atoms. They are divided into two major classes: i) aliphatic, which do not have any aromatic ring, and ii) aromatic, which contain at least one aromatic ring, most often a benzene ring, \(\ce{C6H6}\)  a six-\( \ce{C}\) chain containing alternate single and double bonds. The aliphatic hydrocarbons are divided into three sub-classes: i) alkane containing only single \(\ce{C-C}\) bonds, ii) alkene containing at least one double \(\ce{C=C}\), and alkyne containing at least one triple \(\ce{C \equiv}\) bond, as illustrated in the figure on the right.</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/02:_Representing_organic_structures/2.03:_Organic_nomenclature__-alkanes" class="mt-section" id="section_7"><span id="Straight-chain_alkane"></span><h3 class="editable">Straight-chain alkane</h3>

<section class="box-emphasis">
<p>IUPAC name of a straight chain has to parts: stem name, listed in Table \(\PageIndex{3}\) that tells how many \(\ce{C}\) atoms are in the chain and suffix -ane where -e tells it is a hydrocarbn and -an- tells it is an alkane.</p>
</section>

<p>For example, \(\ce{CH3CH2CH2CH2CH3}\) is pentane, where pent- is the stem name for five-\(\ce{C}\) chain and -ane is the suffix for an alkane. Similarly, \(\ce{CH4}\) is methane, \(\ce{CH3CH3}\) is ethane, \(\ce{CH3CH2CH3}\) is propane, and so on.</p>

