</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/02:_Representing_organic_structures/2.03:_Organic_nomenclature__-alkanes" class="mt-section" id="section_8"><span id="Branched_alkane"></span><h3 class="editable">Branched alkane </h3>

<section class="box-emphasis">
<ol>
    <li>Find the longest continuous \(\ce{C}\)-chain and name it as a straight chain alkane; it is the parent chain in the IUPAC name of the compound.
    <ul>
        <li>If there is a tie, preference goes to the chain with more branches.</li>
    </ul>
    </li>
    <li>Name all of the branches as straight chain alkanes, but with the suffix -ane replaced with -yl for the branch name; list the branches, referred to as <strong>substituents</strong>, alphabetically before the parent name with no spaces between the names.
    <ul>
        <li>If the same branch appears more than once, list it once with a multiplier prefix. The multiplier prefixes are listed in Table \(\PageIndex{5}\).</li>
    </ul>
    </li>
    <li>Number the \(\ce{C}\) atoms of the parent chain from #1 for one end, continuously increasing till the other end, beginning from the end that assigns a lower number to the point of attachment of the first branch.
    <ul>
        <li>If there is a tie, preference goes to the branch that is first in alphabetical order.
        <ul>
            <li>If there is still a tie, the preference goes to the third branch.</li>
        </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>
    </li>
    <li>Insert the locant number, which is the point of attachment of the branch on the parent chain, before the branch name, separated by hyphens from the alphabet.
    <ul>
        <li>If a branch is repeated, list the locant number of each in increasing numeric order, separated by commas before the multiplier prefix.
        <ul>
            <li>If a repeat branch is on the same locant, repeat the locant number. </li>
        </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>
    </li>
    <li>If a branch is further branched, name it by the above four rules with the following changes.
    <ol>
        <li>Numbering of the parent chain in the branch begins from the end that assigns a lower number to the \(\ce{C}\) attached to the parent chain of the compound.</li>
        <li>Suffix -ane is replaced with -yl with a locant number before it, which is the number of the \(\ce{C}\) in the branch that is attached to the parent chain of the compound.</li>
        <li>The entire name of the branch is enclosed in brackets and listed in alphabetical order, along with other branches, preceded by its locant number. </li>
    </ol>
    </li>
</ol>
</section>

