<p>The rules #1 to 5 above are explained through application to the following examples. <img alt="structure of 2-ethyldecane" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488501/clipboard_e061c94a4dec17a32734682b1414b5bf6.png?revision=1" /></p>

<p>The structure shown in the figure on the right is 4-ethyldecane &mdash;numbers in blue show the numbering of the parent chain in this and the following structures. Here is the breakdown of the nomenclature: the parent chain is &quot;decane&quot; (not nonane, rule#1); the branch name is &quot;ethyl&quot; note ethane and listed before the parent &quot;ethyldecane&quot; with no space between the words by rule#2; numbering begins from the end that assigns #4 to the branch (not from the other end that will assign it #7) rule#3, and its final name after applying rule#4 is &quot;4-ethyldecane&quot;.</p>

<p><img alt="clipboard_ec977ee1acb3d32c65db4918152f5d6e0.png" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488502/clipboard_ec977ee1acb3d32c65db4918152f5d6e0.png?revision=1" />The structure in the figure on the left is 3-ethyl-2-methylhexane. Here is the breakdown of the nomenclature: the parent chain is the one with two branches, not the one with one branch (rule#1, if there is a tie, preference goes to the chain with more branches); the branches are listed in alphabetic order (not by the locant number) rule#2, and the locant nuber of each branch is inserted before the branch name (rule#4).</p>

<p><img alt="structure of 3-ethyl-5-methylheptane" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488508/clipboard_eeb5e16648c7f5666fa3bbb1d2eac6e9c.png?revision=1" />The structure in the figure on the right is 3-ethyl-5-methylheptane. Numbering from either side gives #3 to the first substituent. The tie is broken by giving preference to ethyl, which alphabetically comes before methyl (Rule#3).</p>

<p><img alt="structure of 3,3,4-trimethylhexane" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488509/clipboard_e302885bebe9e57f711e6fef88655e8c9.png?revision=1" />The structure on the left is 3,3,4-trimethylhexane. The numbering from either side is the same for the first substituent, so preference is given to the third substituent (rule#3). Methyl substituent repeats three times, so it is listed once with a multiplier of three (Rule#2). Methyl substituent repeats on #3, so the locant number is also repeated (rule#4).</p>

<p><img alt="clipboard_eb8e931a87cba25be59930e52e967c4e3.png" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/437455/clipboard_eb8e931a87cba25be59930e52e967c4e3.png?revision=1" />The structure on the right is 5-(2-methylpentan-3-yl)decane. Numbers in blue represent the numbering of the parent, and numbers in orange represent the numbering in the branch chain. Here is its breakup. The longest chain is ten-\(\ce{C}\) atoms, so the parent name is decane. Numbering of the parent begins from left to assign a lower number to the branch. The branch is complex, so it is named according to rule#5: Numbering from either side assigns #3 to the point of attachment to the parent, so numbering begins from left to give preference to the methyl branch. The prefix -ane is replaced with -yl with locant, i.e., the point of attachment from the branch to the parent is inserted before the prefix (3-yl). The branch name is enclosed in brackets and listed before the parent name, preceded by its locant number on the parent (5-).</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/02:_Representing_organic_structures/2.03:_Organic_nomenclature__-alkanes" class="mt-section" id="section_9"><span id="Cycloalkane"></span><h3 class="editable">Cycloalkane</h3>

<p>Open-chain aliphatic hydrocarbons are called <strong>acyclic</strong>, and those containing a cyclic structure are <strong>cyclic </strong>hydrocarbons. The rules of the acyclic alkanes, but with the following change, name a cycloalkane.</p>

<section class="box-emphasis">
<ol>
    <li>A cycloalkane with no substituent on it has the same name as a straight-chain alkane containing the same number of \(\ce{C}\) atoms, but with the prefix cyclo- attached.</li>
    <li>A cycloalkane with one branch is named by listing the branch name before the parent cycloalkane name, without locant number or space.
    <ul>
        <li>According to the current IUPAC rules, cyclic structure is the parent name, even if the branch has more \(\ce{C}\) atoms, except when there is a characteristic group or other factor that can change the preference. (Note: in earlier versions, between cyclic and acyclic, the one with more \(\ce{C}\) atoms was the parent.</li>
    </ul>
    </li>
    <li>A cycloalkane with two branches is named by listing the branch names in alphabetical order before the parent name, preceded by their locant numbers.
    <ul>
        <li>The branch alphabetically first receives locant #1, and the numbering proceeds clockwise or counterclockwise, whichever assigns a lower number to the second branch.</li>
    </ul>
    </li>
    <li>A cycloalkane with more than two branches is named by listing the branch names in alphabetical order before the parent name, preceded by their locant numbers, where the locant number#1 and the direction to proceed clockwise or counterclockwise is chosen such that the total of the set of locant numbers is the lowest possible.</li>
</ol>
</section>

<p>The application of the above rules is explained with examples in Figure \(\PageIndex{6}\). The structure on the left in Figure \(\PageIndex{6}\) is three-\(\ce{C}\) cycle named cyclopropane by rule#1. The next one has one methyl branch and is named methylcyclohexane by rule#2. The location number for one branch is not needed, as it is always #1. The next structure is named 1-ethyl-3-methylcyclopentane by rule#3; it has two branches, numbering begins from ethyl, which is alphabetically before methyl, and numbering continues counterclockwise to assign a lower number to the methyl branch. The next structure is named 2-ethyl-1,4-dimethylcyclohexane by rule#4: locant of methyl near ethyl is chosen #1 and continued counterclockwise to achieve the lowest total of the set of locant numbers (\(1+2+4 = 7\)). All other options will have a higher total set of locant numbers, e.g., 1-ethyl-2,5-dimethylcyclohexane (\(1+2+5=8\)) and 3-ethyl-1,4-dimethylcyclohexane (\(3+1+4=8\)). The structure on the right in Figure \(\PageIndex{6}\) is named (4-methylhexyl)cyclobutane by rule#4: cyclobutane is the parent instead of hexane because the cyclic structure has preference over acyclic if other factors are the same. The branch is complex, so it is named by rule#5 of branched alkane rules.</p>

