<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/02:_Representing_organic_structures/2.03:_Organic_nomenclature__-alkanes" class="mt-section" id="section_10"><span id="Bicycloalkane"></span><h4 class="editable">Bicycloalkane</h4>

<p>Compounds containing more than one cyclic structure are called polycyclic. Nomenclature of bicycloalkenes, i.e., alkanes containing two cyclic structures, is described here. There are four major classes of bicycloalkanes, as shown in Figure \(\PageIndex{7}\). Isolated rings have no atoms in common. They are named by the rules of alkanes and cycloalkanes, by selecting a larger ring as the parent and the smaller a substituent. For example, the structure on the left in Figure \(\PageIndex{7}\) is cyclobutylcyclopentane.</p>

<figure role="group">
<div class="row">
<figure><img alt="clipboard_ea04dd30d63bd7cc26aadb44b17866909.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488747/clipboard_ea04dd30d63bd7cc26aadb44b17866909.png?revision=1" />cyclobutylcyclopentane</figure>

<figure><img alt="clipboard_e8088351b6b6deba60d256fef291f1075.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488748/clipboard_e8088351b6b6deba60d256fef291f1075.png?revision=1" />spiro[4.2]heptane</figure>

<figure><img alt="clipboard_e23699229f794f9112cb0005aa266b693.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488749/clipboard_e23699229f794f9112cb0005aa266b693.png?revision=1" />bicyclo[4.3.0]nonane</figure>

<figure><img alt="clipboard_e3f3a0a9e0e3bb9979a896318a5507472.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488750/clipboard_e3f3a0a9e0e3bb9979a896318a5507472.png?revision=1" />bicyclo[4.3.2]undecane</figure>
</div>

<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{7}\): Paste Caption Here</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>The second class consists of spiro-rings that share one atom in common. The spiro-rings have two bridges comprising carbons that are not common between the rings. They are named by using the prefix spiro, followed by the number of atoms in each bridge in decreasing numeric order, separated by a period, and enclosed in square brackets, followed by the name of the straight chain alkane corresponding to the total number of carbon atoms in the compound. For example, the structure in Figure \(\PageIndex{7}\), 2nd from left, is spiro[2,4]heptane: 2,4 represents two carbons in the smaller and four in the larger bridge, and heptane because it has a total of seven \(\ce{C}\) atoms. Note that the shared \(\ce{C}\) is not counted in any bridge.</p>

<p>The 3rd major class is fused rings that have two atoms and one covalent bond in common. The common atoms connect the three bridges and are called bridgehead carbons. Their name begins with the prefix bicyclo, followed by the number of atoms in each bridge in decreasing order, separated by a period, and enclosed in square brackets, followed by the name of the straight chain alkane corresponding to the total number of carbon atoms in the compound. For example, the 3rd structure from left in Figure \(\PageIndex{7}\) is bicyclo[4.3.0]nonane. It has four carbons in the largest bridge, three in the second largest, and the middle bridge without any carbon is the smallest, which makes [4.3.0] part of the name, and there are a total of nine carbons, which is represented by nonane in the last part of the name. Note that the bridgehead carbons do not count in any bridge.</p>

<p><img alt="10-methylbicyclo[4,3.2]undecane" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488751/clipboard_e6de34702950f58090fe575a9ceee3829.png?revision=1" /><img alt="8-ethyl-6-methylbicyclo[3.2.1]octane" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488752/clipboard_ee60b130bfe9898aa1fcec64e5b029a4d.png?revision=1" />The 4th class is bridged rings that have more than two atoms in common, i.e., all three bridges have one or more \(\ce{C}\) each. Bridged rings are more common in natural products than the other bicycloalkanes. Their name begins with the prefix bicyclo, followed by the number of atoms in each bridge in decreasing order, separated by a period, and enclosed in square brackets, followed by the name of the straight chain alkane corresponding to the total number of carbon atoms in the compound. for example, structure on the right in Figure \(\PageIndex{7}\) is bicyclo[4.3.2]undecane. Numbering, if needed, starts from a bridgehead, goes through the largest bridge first, followed by the 2nd largest, and finally through the smallest bridge. For example, the figure on the left, which is 1-methylbicyclo[4.3.2]undecane, and the figure on the right, which is 8-ethyl-6-methylbicyclo[3.2.1]octane, show the numbers in blue fonts.</p>

<section class="box-note">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/02:_Representing_organic_structures/2.03:_Organic_nomenclature__-alkanes" class="mt-section"><span id="Primary.2C_secondary.2C_tertiary.2C_and_quaternary_.5C(.5Cce.7BC.7D.5C)_and_.5C(.5Cce.7BH.7D.7C)"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Primary, secondary, tertiary, and quaternary \(\ce{C}\) and \(\ce{H}|)</span></h5>

<p class="lt-chem-394104"><img alt="primary, secondary, tertiary, and quaternary designation for carbon and hydrogen illustrated" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/415936/clipboard_eff8745d7bf27afa823329b90d1903c00.png?revision=1" />The designations primary, secondary, tertiary, and quaternary for carbons and for hydrogens on them are illustrated in the figure on the right, and described below.</p>

<ul>
    <li class="lt-chem-394104">The terminal \(\ce{C}\), i.e., the \(\ce{C}\) with only one single bond with another \(\ce{C}\) is a <strong>primary carbon</strong>.</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-394104">The \(\ce{C}\) with two single bonds to other \(\ce{C}\) atoms is a <strong>secondary carbon</strong>.</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-394104">The \(\ce{C}\) with three single bonds to other \(\ce{C}\) atoms is a <strong>tertiary carbon</strong>. </li>
    <li class="lt-chem-394104">The \(\ce{C}\) with four single bonds to other \(\ce{C}\) atoms is a <strong>quaternary carbon</strong>.</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-394104">A \(\ce{H}\) on a primary \(\ce{C}\) is a primary hydrogen, a \(\ce{H}\) on a secondary \(\ce{C}\) is a secondary hydrogen, and a \(\ce{H}\) on a tertiary \(\ce{C}\) is a tertiary hydrogen. A quaternary \(\ce{C}\) does not have any \(\ce{H}\) on it.</li>
</ul>
</div></section>

<section class="box-note">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/02:_Representing_organic_structures/2.03:_Organic_nomenclature__-alkanes" class="mt-section"><span id="Cis.2Ftrans_isomerism_in_cycloalkane_and_alkene"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Cis/trans isomerism in cycloalkane and alkene</span></h5>

<p><img alt="Free rotation around a single bond" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488642/clipboard_e86a03d4bd9f90d4f9e23824276980611.png?revision=1" />There is almost free rotation possible around a single bond, as shown for \(\ce{\overset{3}{C}-\overset{4}{C}}\) bond of butane in the figure on the right (prepared using <a title="https://chemagic.org/molecules/amini.html" href="https://chemagic.org/molecules/amini.html" target="_blank" rel="external noopener nofollow" class="link-https">https://chemagic.org/molecules/amini.html</a>, Copyright: Public domain). However, the rotation around \(\ce{C-C}\) bonds of a cycloalkane is restricted due to the carbon-bridge connecting the bonds. Rotation around a double bond (\(\ce{C=C}\)) is also restricted because a \(\pi\)-bond weakens when the \(p\)-orbitals are not aligned and breaks completely when they are at a 90&deg; angle from each other. Therefore, two groups on any two \(\ce{C}\) atoms of a cycloalkane ring or on the two \(\ce{C}\) atoms of a \(\ce{C=C}\) pointing in the same direction are called in a <em>cis</em>-relationship, and those pointing in the opposite direction are called in a <em>tra</em>ns-relationship. <img alt="structure of cis and trans 1,2-dimethylcyclopentane" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488649/clipboard_e8dfa4dc21db81b1f1db1599cbcdbca6d.png?revision=1" />For example, <em>cis</em>-1,2-cyclopentane and <em>trans</em>-1,2-cyclopentane, shown in the figure on the right, are different compounds having the same molecular formula, i.e., they are isomers. Such isomers, which have the same atom-connectivity but different orientations, are called stereoisomers. </p>

<p>Stereoisomers need stereodescriptors in their IUPAC nomenclature. The cis/trans were stereodescriptors before, but they have been replaced with new stereodescriptors in the current IUPAC nomenclature. Still, cis/trans terms are used to describe the orientation of two groups relative to each other. The stereodescriptors and the IUPAC nomenclature with the stereodescriptors will be described in the next chapter.</p>
</div></section>

</div></div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/02:_Representing_organic_structures/2.03:_Organic_nomenclature__-alkanes" class="mt-section" id="section_11"><span id="Common_branch_names_accepted_in_IUPAC_nomenclature"></span><h3 class="editable">Common branch names accepted in IUPAC nomenclature</h3>

<p><img alt="structure of isopentylundecane" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488598/clipboard_ebcceb1e8f72411bfbe86b0f282e1f965.png?revision=1" /><img alt="4-isopropyl-5-methyloctane" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488599/clipboard_e5f5d5983325f642d968296b9d6308755.png?revision=1" />Some common names that are often used in literature are accepted in IUPAC nomenclature. They will be mentioned when used in this course. Some alkyl branch names accepted in IUPAC nomenclature are shown in Table \(\PageIndex{7}\). These branch names should be better committed to memory.</p>

<p>For example, 6-(3-methylbutyl)undecane and isopentylundecane are both IUPAC names of the same structure shown on the left, and 4-methyl-5-(propan-2-yl)octane and 4-isopropyl-5-methyloctane are both IUPAC names of the same structure shown on the right.</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table">
    <caption>Table \(\PageIndex{7}\): Common names of some alkyl branches accepted in IUPAC names along with the corresponding systematic branch names.*</caption>
    <tbody>
